BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Civic Meetup #559: Creeped Out: Privacy and Discrimination Risks o
 f Health-related Machine Listening
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260908T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260908T210000
UID:e7360b13cd12d6c422b62a21d9340254@guild.host
DESCRIPTION:New technologies and organizations’ practices are regularly 
 placing pressures and challenges on our privacy and transparency rights. T
 his month\, Civic Tech Toronto and the Information and Privacy Commissione
 r of Ontario are highlighting the work to defend\, enforce\, explain and u
 pdate these rights. To see all September events\, check our events listing
  on Guild.&#x20\;\n\n‎‎\n\nTopic: Creeped Out: Privacy and Discriminat
 ion Risks of Health-related Machine Listening\n\nTypical arguments about c
 reepy technology focus on the visceral ‘ick’ factor of innovation. In 
 contrast\, this talk shares a methodology to track patterns of function cr
 eep and empirically assess changing privacy and discrimination risks. The 
 research focuses on the product development and deployment of health-relat
 ed machine listening: apps and devices that combine audio signal processin
 g and machine learning techniques to produce sensitive inferences about pe
 ople’s health from data points such as vocal biomarkers\, coughs\, infan
 t cries\, and other sounds. Drawing on a 10-year analysis period\, the pre
 sentation will show the escalating privacy risks that are raised as health
 -related machine listening expands into new settings and targets ever-more
  data subjects.\n\nProject Website: https://www.infoscapelab.ca/projects/l
 osingyourvoice\n\n‎‎\n\nSpeaker: Stephen J. Neville \\[in-person]\n\nS
 tephen J. Neville is a media and surveillance scholar based in Guelph\, On
 tario. Currently\, he is a Fellow at the Infoscape Research Lab at Toronto
  Metropolitan University. He was the principal investigator for “Losing 
 your voice to AI”\, funded by the Office of the Privacy Commissioner of 
 Canada (2025-2026)\, which mapped the emerging industry of health-related 
 machine listening to address problems of data privacy\, consent\, and tran
 sparency. With Greg Elmer\, he is the coauthor of The Politics of Media Sc
 arcity (Routledge 2024) which foregrounds marginalized histories and story
 telling as a challenge to dominant narratives of abundance and digital div
 ides in media studies. // Bluesky\n\n‎\n\nAgenda:\n\n7:00-7:20 Welcome a
 nd Introductions\n\n7:20-7:50 Presentation and Q\\&A/discussion\n\n7:50-9:
 00 Breakout groups\n\nCode of Conduct: https://civictech.ca/about-us\n\n
 ‎\n\nCheck in with us on the Civic Tech Toronto Slack: https://link.civi
 ctech.ca/chat\n\n‎\n\nAbout Us:\n\nOur weekly civic meetups bring togeth
 er Torontonians (designers\, coders\, urban planners\, government staff\, 
 mappers\, policy-makers\, students\, communications strategists\, communit
 y organizers\, and more) who share an interest in making Toronto more resp
 onsive\, prosperous\, sustainable\, and equitable through design\, tech\, 
 and data.\n\nCome and be part of it!\n\nFor more info: https://civictech.c
 a\n\n\\--\n\nInterested in speaking at a future event\, or know someone wh
 o'd be a great speaker? We'd love to hear from you:\n\nhttps://link.civict
 ech.ca/speaker-suggestions\n\nHave a space that could host up to 80 civic-
 minded folks for an evening? Consider recommending your venue:\n\nhttps://
 link.civictech.ca/venue-suggestions\n\nVenue Entrances:\n\nFrom Bloor St o
 r subway concourse\, or from Yonge St (just beside Jack Astor's)\n\nBloor-
 01\n\nBloor-02\n\nYonge\n
